1. Dependencies distributed in the container images
====================================================================================================================

The images built from core/Dockerfile and relay/Dockerfile contain the Python packages pinned in core/uv.lock and
relay/uv.lock (without the development group). Each keeps its own licence, whose text is in the package's
*.dist-info directory inside the image. Every one of them is checked to be compatible with AGPL-3.0 before each
export (MIT, BSD-2-Clause, BSD-3-Clause, Apache-2.0, ISC, MPL-2.0, PSF-2.0, LGPL-3.0, and the ones below).

Packages whose licence needs a note:

wordfreq (https://github.com/rspeer/wordfreq)
  Code: Copyright 2022 Robyn Speer. Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0.
  Data: "wordfreq is freely redistributable under the Apache license (see LICENSE.txt), and it includes data
  files that may be redistributed under a Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 license
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/)."
  As its README states, wordfreq contains data extracted from Google Books Ngrams
  (http://books.google.com/ngrams) and Google Books Syntactic Ngrams; data derived from the Leeds Internet Corpus
  (University of Leeds Centre for Translation Studies), Wikipedia (http://www.wikipedia.org) and ParaCrawl
  (https://paracrawl.eu); data from OPUS OpenSubtitles 2018 (http://opus.nlpl.eu/OpenSubtitles.php), originating
  from the OpenSubtitles project (http://www.opensubtitles.org/); and the SUBTLEX word lists SUBTLEX-US,
  SUBTLEX-UK, SUBTLEX-CH, SUBTLEX-DE and SUBTLEX-NL, created by Marc Brysbaert et al.
  (http://crr.ugent.be/programs-data/subtitle-frequencies). SUBTLEX is freely available data; the SUBTLEX authors
  are credited here as wordfreq's terms require. Citation: Robyn Speer (2022). rspeer/wordfreq: v3.0 (v3.0.2).
  Zenodo. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7199437

